  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What does xylem carry?",
      "a": "Xylem carries water and dissolved minerals from the roots up to the stem and leaves. It flows in one direction only, upwards.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about xylem"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Which way does phloem carry sugar?",
      "a": "Phloem carries dissolved sugars, mainly sucrose, from where they are made or stored to where they are needed, such as growing buds, fruits and roots. It can flow up or down.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about fruit and buds",
        "Tell me about phloem"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Is xylem alive?",
      "a": "No. Mature xylem vessels are dead, hollow tubes with no end walls, strengthened by a tough substance called lignin. Phloem is made of living cells.",
      "asks": [
        "Are phloem cells alive?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does water get into the roots?",
      "a": "Water enters root hair cells by osmosis, because the cell sap is more concentrated than the soil water. It then passes across the root to the xylem.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the roots"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a vascular bundle?",
      "a": "A vascular bundle is a group of xylem and phloem tubes running side by side. You can see them as the stringy bits in celery and as veins in leaves.",
      "asks": [
        "What are the strings in celery?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Xylem vessels are dead, hollow tubes strengthened with lignin; phloem tubes are living cells.",
    "The movement of sugars through phloem is called translocation."
  ],
